# Quick Commit Rule

## Purpose
Quickly commit modified files with an intelligent commit message.

## Implementation

```bash
# Check for modified files only
if [ -z "$(git diff --name-only)" ]; then
    echo "No changes to commit"
    exit 0
fi

# Stage only modified files (not untracked)
git add -u

# Generate commit message based on file changes
if git diff --cached --name-only | grep -q "asymmetric_hrm.py"; then
    if git diff --cached asymmetric_hrm.py | grep -q "H_to_L_proj\|H_module"; then
        MSG="fix: correct HRM architecture - move projections and H processing outside T_cycles loop"
    elif git diff --cached asymmetric_hrm.py | grep -q "def\|class"; then
        MSG="feat: add new features and improve code structure"
    else
        MSG="refactor: improve code quality and structure"
    fi
elif git diff --cached --name-only | grep -q "JOURNAL.md\|SNAPSHOT.md"; then
    MSG="docs: update project documentation and tracking"
else
    MSG="fix: general improvements and updates"
fi

# Add file details
FILES=$(git diff --cached --name-only | tr '\n' ' ')
MSG="$MSG

Files: $FILES"

# Commit
git commit -m "$MSG"
```

## Usage
Run this rule when you want to quickly commit all modified files with an appropriate message.

## Features
- Only commits modified files (not untracked)
- Generates intelligent commit messages based on file types
- Handles common change patterns
- Quick and simple execution